The error comes from assigning to the `IN` parameter:

`p_tag := UPPER(TRIM(p_tag));`

In PL/SQL, an `IN` parameter is read-only inside the subprogram, so it cannot be used as an assignment target. To keep the published parameter mode as `IN`, introduce a local variable and assign the normalized value to that variable instead.

```plsql
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE normalize_tag(p_tag IN VARCHAR2) IS
  l_tag p_tag%TYPE;
BEGIN
  l_tag := UPPER(TRIM(p_tag));
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(l_tag);
END normalize_tag;
```

This proposal has not been compiled. It changes only the assignment target and the value passed to `DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE`; the parameter remains `IN`. If this procedure is actually a member of a package, the same change must be made inside the complete package body rather than as a standalone procedure.

