```plsql
CREATE OR REPLACE TRIGGER parcel_limit
FOR INSERT ON parcels
COMPOUND TRIGGER

  TYPE t_route_ids IS TABLE OF parcels.route_id%TYPE;
  l_route_ids t_route_ids := t_route_ids();

  BEFORE STATEMENT IS
  BEGIN
    l_route_ids := t_route_ids();
  END BEFORE STATEMENT;

  BEFORE EACH ROW IS
  BEGIN
    l_route_ids.EXTEND;
    l_route_ids(l_route_ids.LAST) := :NEW.route_id;
  END BEFORE EACH ROW;

  AFTER STATEMENT IS
    l_count NUMBER;
  BEGIN
    FOR i IN 1 .. l_route_ids.COUNT LOOP
      SELECT COUNT(*)
        INTO l_count
        FROM parcels
       WHERE route_id = l_route_ids(i);

      IF l_count > 50 THEN
        RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20351, 'route capacity');
      END IF;
    END LOOP;
  END AFTER STATEMENT;

END parcel_limit;
/
```

The error comes from this line in the original row-level trigger:

```sql
SELECT COUNT(*) INTO l_count FROM parcels WHERE route_id = :NEW.route_id;
```

Because the trigger is `BEFORE INSERT ON parcels FOR EACH ROW`, Oracle marks `HARNESS_APP.PARCELS` as mutating while each row is being inserted. A row-level trigger on that table cannot query the same table, so the `SELECT COUNT(*)` raises:

```text
ORA-04091: table HARNESS_APP.PARCELS is mutating, trigger/function may not see it
```

The redesign moves the count check out of the per-row section and into an `AFTER STATEMENT` section. The trigger still rejects inserts that would leave more than 50 parcels for a route, but it does so after the statement has finished modifying the table, which avoids the mutating-table restriction.

One important limitation remains: this trigger prevents capacity violations within a single SQL statement, but it does not by itself prevent two concurrent sessions from both passing the check and then both committing. If strict capacity enforcement across concurrent inserts is required, the application or database design needs an additional serialization mechanism, such as locking a parent route row before inserting parcels, if such a parent table exists.

The reported `ORA-01001: invalid cursor` comes from the explicit cursor being fetched before it is opened.

In the supplied unit, the cursor is declared as:

```text
CURSOR c IS SELECT 23 AS reading FROM dual;
```

but the executable section begins with:

```text
FETCH c INTO l_value;
```

An explicit PL/SQL cursor must be opened before `FETCH`. The missing statement is `OPEN c;`, and it belongs immediately before the `FETCH` on line 5 of the selected source.

I also noticed the supplied user message asks to “return the row for sensor 41”, but the context does not show a sensor table, sensor identifier column, or any object that could be used to return a row for sensor 41. I cannot write that part without inventing objects or columns. The repair below fixes the actual compiler/runtime error while preserving the explicit cursor usage and leaving the rest of the supplied code unchanged.

```plsql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION sensor_value RETURN NUMBER IS
  CURSOR c IS SELECT 23 AS reading FROM dual;
  l_value NUMBER;
BEGIN
  OPEN c;
  FETCH c INTO l_value;
  CLOSE c;
  RETURN l_value;
END sensor_value;
```

